# How to cite OpenChip.
#
# Read by GitHub (which renders a "Cite this repository" button) and by several
# reference managers. Format: Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# Zenodo also reads this file when it archives a GitHub release, so the authors,
# ORCID, title and licence here become the metadata of the minted DOI.
#
# One field is deliberately absent:
#
#   doi              None minted yet. Zenodo mints one per release; add the
#                    concept DOI (the one that always resolves to the latest
#                    version) once the first release exists.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If OpenChip designed a device that appears in your paper, please cite the
  software and state the version. The geometry generator and the design-check
  thresholds change between versions.

title: "OpenChip: browser-based microfluidic chip design for resin printing"

abstract: >-
  OpenChip is a browser-based tool for designing microfluidic mixing chips and
  exporting them as STL files for desktop resin (mSLA) printing. Users draw a 2D
  channel network, configure ports and mixing chambers, generate a solid CAD
  model, check the design against their printer's resolution, estimate the flow
  analytically, and export STL, SVG, OpenSCAD or images. It runs entirely
  client-side with no backend.

type: software

authors:
  - family-names: Cimoca
    given-names: Julian
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-8978-5337"
    email: info@openchip.tech

license: MIT

version: 1.0.0

url: "https://openchip.tech"

repository-code: "https://github.com/Jujified/OpenChip"

keywords:
  - microfluidics
  - lab-on-a-chip
  - 3D printing
  - stereolithography
  - mSLA
  - CAD
  - laminar flow
  - open hardware
